Install a repository-local, deterministic multi-agent coordination layer for AETHAR/AETHERIA so an operator can tell independent chat agents to “work on the next issue” without duplicate implementation, branch reuse, hidden file overlap, uncontrolled scope expansion or premature completion claims.

This is repository-work coordination only. It must not weaken or replace AETHERIA engineering authority, evidence boundaries, schema/fixture truth, photometry/manufacturing restrictions or release controls.

Central board: #114.

## Why this issue exists

AETHERIA already has a proven project-specific Agent CTO kernel in `.aether/FAST-KERNEL.md`, but it has no enforced GitHub issue-ownership lifecycle comparable to the newer LEAPH/CLEO/RAAS systems. Open implementation lanes currently rely on human discipline and PR visibility rather than deterministic ownership.

Use LEAPH `harness-claims/v2` from `ruddvz/leaf` PR #67 as the reference implementation **only after its remaining source blocker is resolved and the protocol is validated**. Do not copy LEAPH engineering ontology or active ownership state.

## Required architecture

### 1. Repository-local authority

- Issue comments are the authoritative ownership event log.
- #114 is a derived board, never the source of ownership truth.
- AETHERIA controlled sources remain engineering/product authority.
- Cross-repository federation may share generic algorithms and failure modes only.

### 2. One issue, one mutation owner

A valid implementation claim must bind:
- stable agent ID;
- unique branch;
- exact bounded scope;
- files/globs;
- shared interfaces/contracts when relevant;
- GitHub actor/claimant identity where the protocol can establish it.

First valid unreleased claim wins. Later claims do not gain ownership by repetition.

### 3. Cross-issue collision prevention

Enforce or fail closed on:
- duplicate active ownership of one issue;
- one branch claimed by multiple active issues;
- overlapping files/globs where an earlier owner exists;
- declared shared-interface conflicts where two lanes can break the same contract even if files differ;
- PR changed paths outside the accepted claim, including old/new paths for renames;
- branch/base drift that invalidates the preflight assumptions;
- legacy open PR ownership during bootstrap.

Do not treat different issue numbers as proof of independence.

### 4. Claim integrity

Adopt the hardened lessons from LEAPH rather than the simplest Garba parser:
- deterministic parsing;
- actor-bound accepted claims when identity is available;
- safe same-owner narrowing and heartbeat semantics;
- no automatic claim expiry;
- advisory stale-claim review only;
- explicit owner override/reassign path;
- safe handling of edited/deleted claim comments so history cannot silently free or broaden ownership;
- idempotent webhook/redelivery behaviour;
- atomic reassignment must bind the successor actor, branch and equal-or-narrower resources and must not leave lifecycle control with the previous owner.

### 5. Issue quality contract

Add an agent-ready implementation issue template. Every implementation issue intended for autonomous agents should contain, where applicable:
- outcome/user or engineering problem;
- why it matters;
- controlling source(s) of truth;
- exact in-scope work;
- explicit non-goals;
- dependencies/blockers;
- expected file/interface ownership;
- acceptance criteria;
- validation commands/evidence required;
- UI/UX/accessibility/browser checks when visual/interactive;
- safety/engineering/evidence restrictions when controlled domains are touched;
- rollout/live verification requirements when public/deployed;
- rollback/recovery considerations for persistent or destructive changes;
- completion condition.

A broad master/programme issue must be split into bounded child implementation issues before parallel mutation.

### 6. Critic/reviewer separation

For medium/high-risk or engineering-significant work, the implementing agent must not be the only acceptance authority.

- deterministic tests/validators remain primary evidence;
- independent engineering/reviewer pass is required where `.aether/FAST-KERNEL.md` or repository policy requires it;
- critic findings inside the accepted lane must be fixed and re-verified;
- valid out-of-scope findings become new deduplicated issues rather than silent scope creep;
- review agents are read-only unless they separately claim a repair issue.

### 7. Autonomous “next issue” selection

When an operator asks an agent to continue through the backlog, finishing one lane must return to fresh preflight.

A safe next issue is:
- open;
- implementation-ready rather than a broad programme/evidence-only external gate;
- dependency-unblocked;
- unclaimed;
- free of file/interface ownership conflicts;
- compatible with the agent’s authority/risk capability;
- higher priority than other equally safe work according to repository policy.

Every new lane gets a fresh claim and branch. Ownership never carries over from the previous issue.

An agent that cannot find a safe mutation lane should stop with exact blockers or take a clearly marked `research-only` issue. It must not manufacture work merely to stay busy.

### 8. Definition of done

A lane is not complete at “code written” or “PR opened”. The lifecycle is:

`preflight -> claim -> implement -> focused validation -> critique/review -> repair -> reconcile current base -> full required validation -> PR -> CI/review -> merge when authorised -> public/live verification where applicable -> issue reconciliation -> release claim -> fresh next-issue selection`

If repository policy or the operator sets an earlier stop point, report that stop truthfully rather than calling the issue complete.

### 9. WIP and concurrency

- Default one active mutation lane per agent.
- Read-only reviewers/research can run in parallel when they reserve no files.
- Parallel mutation is allowed only across demonstrably non-overlapping issue

